Template: fleetwright/coordinator-url
Type: string
Default:
Description: Coordinator URL to join:
 The address of the fleet this box joins, e.g. https://fleet.example.com. It
 is the same address that follows `curl -fsSL` in the one-line installer.
 .
 Leave it blank to install without joining. Join later with:
 sudo fleetwright join fleet.example.com

Template: fleetwright/pin
Type: password
Description: Enrolment pin:
 Six digits from the app (Fleet, then Add a host), or from anyone who has the
 admin token. It is spent once and not kept.
 .
 Blank is fine: enrol whenever you have one with
 sudo -u <service user> fleetwright-sidecar enrol <pin>

Template: fleetwright/user
Type: string
Default:
Description: Account the sessions run as:
 Blank means the account the services already run as, or on a first install
 the one that ran sudo. Asked only at low priority; preseed it for an
 unattended install.

Template: fleetwright/upgrades
Type: boolean
Default: true
Description: Allow system updates from chat?
 The app and the chat commands can then show what the operating system has
 waiting and install it (and install this package alone when a new version
 is in apt). It is one sudoers rule letting the service user start three
 root-owned units: apt-get update, apt-get upgrade with fleetwright held,
 and apt-get install --only-upgrade fleetwright. Nothing else.
 .
 Later: sudo fleetwright grant upgrades on|off

Template: fleetwright/reboot
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Allow reboot from chat?
 /reboot can then restart this machine from the app, behind three
 confirmations: the command, a one-time pin, and the hostname typed out.
 EVERY RUNNING SESSION DIES, which is why it is a separate question and
 defaults to no. It is one sudoers rule: systemctl reboot, and nothing else.
 .
 Later: sudo fleetwright grant reboot on|off
